  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule exist, and the rule is enabled.\n\n**Data exploration:** The `Kibana Sample Data Logs` view (index `kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`) holds web access logs. The fields I used are `clientip`, `bytes`, `response` (a string, so `response.keyword` is the filterable form), `request`/`url`, `geo.*`, `machine.os`, and `agent`.\n\n**Dashboard: `EFF-c87c2862 Web logs overview`**\n- Dashboard ID: `eff-c87c2862-web-logs-overview-dc21f73883c5`\n- Five panels:\n  - Total requests (metric, count)\n  - Requests over time (area, date histogram)\n  - Responses by status code (bar, top 10 `response.keyword` values)\n  - Bytes transferred over time (line, sum of `bytes`)\n  - 5xx responses (ES|QL metric, `response.keyword LIKE \"5*\"`)\n- Time range is set to the last year. I didn't check the sample data's timestamps, so if the panels look empty, widen the range.\n\n**Alert rule: `EFF-c87c2862 5xx spike`**\n- Rule ID: `ab1cbad3-7a60-4926-9462-8e654e151ea7`. Type `.es-query`, consumer `stackAlerts`, runs every 1m.\n- Counts documents where `response.keyword` matches `5*` over a 5-minute window, and fires when the count is greater than 10.\n- Status: enabled. Alerting health reports `ok`.\n\n**Choices I made:**\n- The 5xx match uses a wildcard (`5*`), not a numeric range, because `response` is a string field. I couldn't confirm the exact status-code values: my direct Elasticsearch queries were rejected with a 401, though the Kibana API key works.\n- I created the rule disabled and then enabled it, so the rule's enabled state was set explicitly.\n- I haven't confirmed that the rule has evaluated yet, or that the 5xx panel shows a non-zero count. Both would be worth a quick look in the Kibana UI.",
